%%%%%Board 2
https://tinyurl.com/2dzy4hrk
qx|A2|st||rh||ah|Board 2 IMPs   -   July 31, 2024|pn|hondo717,danpsych,IraCT,Sputniks|
md|4ST753HK985432D76C,SAQJ842H7DQTC8753,S6HAJT6D432CAQ964,SK9HQDAKJ985CKJT2|sv|n|
mb|1D|mb|3H|mb|3S|mb|4Hp|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is not clear what East should bid which is why you preempt. 5@D could be right, but 4@S might be better. It is hard to know. Pass by East should be a ^*BForcing Pass^*n situation, meaning if East passes, West must either double or bid again.|
PG| ***** 13 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Forcing pass situations occur when you are in a game forcing auction and the opponents sacrifice. 3@S is a game forcing bid pretty much.|
PG| ***** 14 ***** |


mb|P|mb|4S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|5H|mb|P|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Again we have a forcing pass situation. West cannot pass. West must double or bid 5@S.  I will discuss what I would do after West makes a decision.|
PG| ***** 15 ***** |


mb|P|mb|5S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Here is what West must consider. If East was very short in spades, they would probably double because they do not want partner to bid 5@S. So by inference has at least mild spade support. However, ^*Bthe 5 level belongs to the opponents^*n is always in play. I probably double, but it is far from clear what to do. Again 5 level decisions in a competitive auction are extremely difficult and even experts often get them wrong.|
PG| ***** 16 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|HA|pc|HQ|pc|H2|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at| My spot card here is a suit preference. When there is a singleton on the board, that is the case. So if I play a low heart, I want a club, if I play a high heart I want a diamond, and if I play a middle heart it means no suit preference.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


